PART 1: GAME IDENTITY AND VISION

1.1 Game identity

Game name: Empires of Akanu.

Tagline: Rise. Conquer. Rule.

Genre:

- Real-time strategy (RTS).
- Historical-inspired empire building.
- Kingdom management.
- Economic simulation.
- Military strategy.
- Diplomacy and territorial conquest.
- Multiplayer strategy.

Primary platforms:

- Desktop browsers.
- Android browsers.
- Tablets.

The game should be installable as a Progressive Web App (PWA), where supported.

1.2 Core vision

Empires of Akanu is a premium African-inspired historical strategy game in which players establish kingdoms, develop settlements, manage populations, gather resources, construct cities, recruit armies, engage in diplomacy and wage wars against rival kingdoms.

The game combines two major gameplay experiences:

1. Persistent kingdom management inspired by games such as Rise of Kingdoms.
2. Direct real-time military strategy inspired by Age of Empires.

The player must be able to develop a small settlement into a powerful kingdom and eventually a continental empire.

The game must make players feel that they are governing a living civilisation rather than simply clicking buttons to increase numbers.

Every important action should have consequences in the game world.

Villagers should work, soldiers should move, buildings should be constructed, markets should operate, armies should march and kingdoms should respond to political and military developments.

1.3 Artistic and cultural direction

The game is inspired by the historical diversity, architecture, clothing, military traditions, landscapes and material cultures of different African societies.

Africa must not be represented as one homogeneous civilisation.

Different kingdoms must have distinguishable:

- Architecture.
- Clothing.
- Military equipment.
- Hairstyles.
- Decorations.
- Settlement designs.
- Cultural motifs.
- Environmental characteristics.
- Visual identities.

Use culturally thoughtful historical inspiration without falsely presenting fictional kingdoms as actual historical states.

The world and its kingdoms are fictional.

