PART 19: DEVELOPMENT RULES

These rules apply throughout the project.

Rule 1: Preserve existing functionality

Before changing existing code, inspect its dependencies and behaviour.

Do not casually delete, replace or rewrite working systems.

Make focused changes.

Rule 2: Implement, do not merely describe

When instructed to develop a system, implement the actual functionality.

Do not substitute explanations, mockups, placeholder buttons or static illustrations for working features.

Rule 3: Verify before claiming success

Test implementations.

Report:

- What works.
- What partially works.
- What does not work.
- What remains incomplete.

Rule 4: No fake functionality

Do not create buttons that appear functional but have no meaningful implementation.

Do not simulate multiplayer using disconnected local players and describe it as online multiplayer.

Do not pretend AI kingdoms are intelligent when they are simply following static scripts.

Rule 5: No unnecessary external dependencies

Prefer stable, suitable and maintainable libraries.

Verify package compatibility before introducing dependencies.

Rule 6: No manual asset-production requirements

The user must not be required to manually model, rig, animate, texture or compose assets in external software.

Use procedural generation and available integrated capabilities.

If a required capability is genuinely unavailable, explain the limitation and implement the best feasible alternative.

Rule 7: No unapproved expenses

Do not enable paid APIs, subscriptions, infrastructure or services without explicit approval.

Estimate expected costs before introducing potentially billable services.

Rule 8: Maintainable code

Use:

- TypeScript types.
- Reusable components.
- Clear module boundaries.
- Consistent naming.
- Error handling.
- Documentation for complex systems.
- Automated tests where appropriate.

Avoid unnecessary code duplication.

Rule 9: Continuous testing

After implementing a major system:

- Run the application.
- Inspect runtime errors.
- Test interactions.
- Test integration.
- Verify mobile behaviour.
- Correct discovered problems before building dependent systems.

Rule 10: Honest technical decisions

Do not invent API capabilities, package functionality, model availability or backend behaviour.

Investigate actual capabilities before using them.

